+# Visual question generation (v1.2.0)
+
+## Contract
+Add optional `visualization` to `Question`, independent of `cognitiveType`, `answerFormat`, and `grading`. The visualization is an aid or stimulus, not a cognitive type. Use a structured, reproducible data model; never make the only source of truth an opaque image.
+
+```json
+{
+  "visualization": {
+    "type": "binary_tree",
+    "data": {
+      "nodes": [{"id":"n50","value":50},{"id":"n30","value":30},{"id":"n70","value":70}],
+      "edges": [{"from":"n50","to":"n30","label":"left"},{"from":"n50","to":"n70","label":"right"}]
+    },
+    "interactive": false,
+    "altText": "Root 50 with left child 30 and right child 70"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+## Supported types and required data
+- `function_plot`: expressions, axes, bounds, units, domain restrictions. Use exact mathematical notation and appropriate plotting tools.
+- `line_chart`, `bar_chart`, `scatter_plot`: named axes, units, explicit numerical data points, series names; distinguish illustrative from measured data.
+- `binary_tree`: unique node IDs, values, left/right edges, explicit root; enforce no cycles, at most two children, one parent per nonroot node. If claiming BST, verify ordering invariant.
+- `directed_graph`, `network_graph`: node IDs, directed/undirected edges, weights if relevant; verify connectivity and paths when required by the question.
+- `flowchart`: node IDs, operation/decision types, labeled branches, entry/exit, valid transitions.
+- `state_diagram`: states, initial state, transitions, events/guards; verify reachable states and transition behavior.
+
+## Workflow
+1. Determine whether a visual improves the objective (e.g. tracing a BST, reading a chart, interpreting network topology). Avoid decorative visuals.
+2. Generate the underlying structured data first, then derive both the visible representation and grading answer from the same data.
+3. Independently validate the answer and visual consistency; reject ambiguous node labels, missing axes/units, impossible graphs, and visual leaks of the answer.
+4. Render with available native visualization tools, SVG, or Mermaid; fall back to text-based diagrams or adjacency lists. Do not assume a tool is installed.
+5. Present alt text or equivalent accessible description. Do not reveal hidden answer keys.
+6. Grade using existing methods; for node-selection or drag interactions, only claim interactivity if supported, otherwise request IDs or ordered sequences as text.
+7. On retry, vary topology, values, labels, or scale while preserving the learning objective.
+
+## Examples
+- Tracing: show a BST; ask for postorder sequence. Compute answer via traversal over node-edge data.
+- Application: show a weighted network; ask which route is shortest. Verify using a shortest-path algorithm and state assumptions about direction/weights.
+- Calculation: show a plotted function and ask for an exact value only if its coordinates are unambiguous; otherwise provide the expression or data table.
+- Conceptual: show a flowchart; ask why a branch is taken for a given input.
+
+Visual interaction is not guaranteed by this skill; the host UI determines actual interactive capabilities.
